Executive Summary
This project simulated a DevSecOps secret-auditing workflow on an Ubuntu Server host within a isolated home lab environment. 
The objective was to implement automated scanning tools to identify exposed API credentials, debug scanner behavior, and establish remediation steps to prevent credential leaks in production environments.

Key Components & Topology
Host Platform: Linux Mint

Target Environment: VirtualBox Ubuntu Server VM

Connectivity: SSH over NAT with port forwarding (Host: 2222 -> Guest: 22)

Target File: ~/ai_app/config.env containing simulated high-entropy API credentials

Tooling Evaluated: TruffleHog (v3.97.4) and Gitleaks (v8.30.1)

Workflow & Technical Execution
Environment & Service Preparation

Identified local server user accounts (rossdboli) and established non-root operational workflows.

Installed and enabled the OpenSSH daemon (openssh-server), configuring UFW firewall rules (sudo ufw allow ssh) to expose port 22 inside the guest system.

Secret Scanner Deployment

Deployed TruffleHog v3 binary to /usr/local/bin using official install scripts.

Compiled and installed Gitleaks directly via release binary downloads after identifying snap package limitations on the platform.

Target File Provisioning

Created a dedicated project directory (~/ai_app) and populated a mock configuration file (config.env) with structured, high-entropy test strings (e.g., sk-proj-...).



Technical Challenges & Workarounds
APT Frontend Database Locks: Resolved background updater collisions (unattended-upgr) by stopping systemctl services, terminating locked process IDs (kill -9 4233), and manually clearing /var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend.

TruffleHog Update Permission Errors: Handled elevated permission constraints by appending --no-update flags to bypass auto-updater routines.

CLI Syntax & Argument Parsing Errors: Resolved TruffleHog lstat false errors caused by boolean flag positional parsing by enforcing strict command ordering (trufflehog --no-update --no-verification filesystem ~/ai_app).

Active vs. Offline Scanner Validation:

TruffleHog v3: Designed primarily for active API verification; suppressed unverified fake keys during offline execution.

Gitleaks: Selected as the primary offline regex scanner (gitleaks dir ~/ai_app -v), successfully matching regex rules against high-entropy target keys.



Findings & Detection Results
Gitleaks successfully flagged the exposed credential with the following audit telemetry:

Rule ID: generic-api-key

Target File: /home/rossdboli/ai_app/config.env (Line 1)

